--TEST--
Core\Signature::sign answers one token for a payload written in two orders, and the same token again for the same payload, because what is signed is a canonical document and never assembled text — `rule:core-api/signing-is-over-a-payload`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The invariance shape, over the property this whole class exists for. Every
// hand-rolled signing helper assembles `"$user|$role|$doc"` and then signs two
// different strings for one payload, depending only on the order the map was
// built in; `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/signature.rs` sorts the keys at every level
// and writes each value with the tag of its own type, so there is no assembly
// for the two sides of a signature to disagree about.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];

array<string> $written = ["user" => "ada", "role" => "admin", "doc" => "7"];
array<string> $shuffled = ["doc" => "7", "user" => "ada", "role" => "admin"];

string $one = Core\Signature::sign($written, {keys: $ring, until: null});
string $two = Core\Signature::sign($shuffled, {keys: $ring, until: null});
echo $one == $two ? "one token" : "two tokens", "\n";

// And a signature is deterministic where a seal is not: `Core\SignedCookie`
// draws a nonce per call, so the same payload seals differently every time,
// while the same payload signs identically every time. That is what lets a
// signed URL be compared, cached and put in a page twice.
string $again = Core\Signature::sign($written, {keys: $ring, until: null});
echo $one == $again ? "and again" : "differs", "\n";

// A payload that differs in one octet is a different document, so the token
// moves — the invariance above is over key *order* and nothing else.
array<string> $elevated = ["user" => "ada", "role" => "root", "doc" => "7"];
string $other = Core\Signature::sign($elevated, {keys: $ring, until: null});
echo $one == $other ? "unchanged" : "a different payload signs differently", "\n";
--EXPECT--
one token
and again
a different payload signs differently
